{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_84","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"rg -n '\\\\b_t\\\\b|this\\\\.effect|this\\\\.battery|cheated = true|options.dev|setup.practice|firePointer' src/view.js src/app.js src/ui.js && tail -5 docs/biome.log && sed -n '17,50p' docs/testing.md\"","aggregated_output":"src/ui.js:93:\t\t\t\tthis.sim.state.cheated = true;\nsrc/ui.js:234:\t\tthis.setup.practice = null;\nsrc/ui.js:632:\t\t\t\tthis.sim.state.cheated = true;\nsrc/ui.js:640:\t\tthis.sim.state.cheated = true;\nsrc/ui.js:692:\t\t\tthis.sim.state.cheated = true;\nsrc/ui.js:1008:\t\t\t\tthis.touch.firePointer = e.pointerId;\nsrc/app.js:252:\ttouch.firePointer = null;\nsrc/app.js:279:\tif (options.dev || JSON.stringify(sim.definition) !== JSON.stringify(MISSION))\nsrc/app.js:280:\t\tsim.state.cheated = true;\nsrc/app.js:294:\t\tsim.state.cheated = true;\nsrc/app.js:805:\tif (touch.firePointer === e.pointerId) {\nsrc/app.js:807:\t\ttouch.firePointer = null;\nsrc/app.js:811:\tif (touch.firePointer === e.pointerId) {\nsrc/app.js:813:\t\ttouch.firePointer = null;\nsrc/app.js:882:\t\t\t\t\tsim.state.cheated = true;\nsrc/view.js:586:\t\tfor (const e of s.effects || []) this.effect(pen, e, t);\nsrc/view.js:669:\t\t\telse if (q.id === \"battery\") this.battery(p, q, _t);\nsrc/view.js:864:\tbattery(p, q, _t) {\nsrc/view.js:1410:\teffect(p, e, _t) {\nChecked 18 files in 108ms. Fixed 2 files.\nFinal simulation run at this stage: **141 checks, zero failures**, through `node tools/verify.mjs --suite=sim`. The edge suite contributes 87 checks and the nine whole-sortie combinations contribute 54. Every combination completed all mission beats, saw all four bearings, fired guns, encountered flak, deliberately released bombs, observed impact, destroyed the primary and safely recovered without invulnerability or infinite resources. Regressions verify that an occupied-seat exchange immediately grants command to the new pilot, repeated same-station requests preserve aim without radio/event flooding, and the dogleg really reduces coastal barrage cadence. After the authored recovery correction, all nine full sorties additionally assert the physical runway endpoint at east −1800 / south 0 / altitude 80 units, stopped airspeed, fully deployed gear and airborne=false before debrief. Structured evidence is in `docs/simulation-results.json`.\n\n| Loadout | Difficulty | Active seconds | Fuel left | Hull left | Fighter kills |\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Standard | Cadet | 590 | 46% | 44.1% | 5 |\n| Standard | Crew | 590 | 39% | 20.1% | 5 |\n| Standard | Veteran | 646 | 24% | 7.7% | 4 |\n| Long Legs | Cadet | 635 | 49% | 44.8% | 4 |\n| Long Legs | Crew | 635 | 43% | 21.1% | 4 |\n| Long Legs | Veteran | 613 | 43% | 4.3% | 4 |\n| Heavy Hand | Cadet | 624 | 39% | 45.6% | 5 |\n| Heavy Hand | Crew | 624 | 32% | 22.2% | 5 |\n| Heavy Hand | Veteran | 602 | 31% | 4.6% | 5 |\n\nEach used two bombs; one direct hit destroyed the ship and the subsequent bomb was scored as wasted ordnance. Durations establish roughly 10–11 active minutes for this scripted first-run route. Browser UI/renderer, touch/pad stand-ins, portable package and Hub harness checks are owned by the main integration work and must be recorded separately before claiming them.\n\n## Issue ledger\n\nTwo simulation implementation issues were discovered during review and communicated before the first edge run: the initial flak danger radius exceeded controllable displacement within its reaction window; replay's one-shot warning cache survived reset. The main builder fixed both, and explicit regression checks now pass for evasion and repeated warning delivery. Neither prevented the whole game's startup. A stale queued nonurgent audio caption surviving an urgent interruption was also corrected during audio review.\n\nThe first whole-sortie bot attempt missed its release window because the test bot recommitted route while retaining its own held bank command; normal direct-input behavior canceled course hold. The bot now neutralizes its controls before ordering course hold. This was a test-driver error, not a game defect, and is excluded from game issue totals.\n\nThe first native-input browser bot destroyed the primary and four fighters but did not evade return flak or use its extinguisher, so it lost the aircraft on return. The integrating builder extended that input script with the same defensive decisions as the successful simulation bot. The corrected run passed **8/8 browser-playthrough assertions** at **619 active seconds**, with **32% fuel**, **86% hull**, **four fighters**, **two bombs**, and **sixteen actual keyboard evasion moves**, without cheats. The failed initial script was an incomplete fixture and is excluded from game issue totals. The resulting recovery review discovered that debrief could precede physical runway touchdown; authored recovery interpolation now corrects this, and the subsequent nine-sortie physical aircraft-state regressions all pass.\n\nMost helpful simulation test: the complete nine-combination operation, because it ties flight, ownership, aiming, damage, finite ordnance, target consequences and return reserves together. Isolated formula checks are useful for exact impact math but cannot establish playability by themselves.\n\n## Integration audit follow-up\n\nThe read-only requirement audit found pilot-look plumbing, fine/coarse sight presentation, persistent independent voice volume, free-camera input wiring, missing touch firing/jettison paths, local Player2 map command identity, indirect pilot command transfer during seat exchange, repeated station-request radio flooding, and the dogleg's initially cosmetic risk description. These were communicated to their owning implementers and corrected in source. The simulation regressions above cover the last three authoritative rules. The actual browser suites must verify the UI/input/rendered consequences before those are counted as release evidence. Pointer capture/cancellation for the emergency jettison hold was also raised, because release outside its original button must cancel the irreversible hold.\n\nGuest gunfire sound was an additional confirmed integration issue: mount shots rendered and consumed ammo, but the transport's intentionally trimmed event stream omitted their audio. The monotonic-counter presentation fallback and regression checks described above correct that without changing authority or packet rate.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
