{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_133","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat /home/micha/.claude/skills/app-platforms/SKILL.md'","aggregated_output":"---\nname: app-platforms\ndescription: Mobile + desktop app toolchain on this box (Android SDK/emulator/android CLI, Flutter, React Native/Expo, Kotlin/Compose, Tauri, Electron, .NET/Avalonia/MAUI, Windows .exe from WSL, iOS via cloud) and its WSL gotchas — read before building, running or testing any mobile or desktop app\n---\n\n# Mobile & desktop app toolchain (installed + verified end to end 2026-09-23 — do NOT reinstall)\n\nEverything is userspace and on PATH in every shell via `~/.config/ai-dev/env.sh`. If a tool seems\nmissing you are in a stale shell: run it through `bash -lc '...'`. Every command below was run for\nreal on this box (build → install → launch → screenshot).\n\n## Hard limits of this box (WSL2, no GUI, 16 GB RAM)\n\n- **No Linux GUI** (WSLg disabled). Drive UIs headlessly: Android emulator `-no-window` + screenshots;\n  Linux desktop apps under `xvfb-run -a` (\"DRI3 / WebGL blocklisted\" warnings there are normal).\n  **Never launch GUI `.exe`s** via interop — they open on the user's Windows desktop. Console `.exe`s\n  are fine (they print to your terminal).\n- **iOS / macOS builds are impossible locally** (need Xcode). Use cloud macOS: **Expo EAS Build**\n  (`eas build -p ios`), GitHub Actions `macos-latest` runners (`gh workflow run`), or Codemagic.\n  Flutter/RN/KMP code is still written + unit-tested here; only the Apple build/sign step is remote.\n- **RAM:** emulator ≈ 2–3 GB, a Gradle build ≈ 2–4 GB, rustc/linking ≈ 1–2 GB. One Gradle build at a\n  time (`--max-workers=8`), `./gradlew --stop` after. `/tmp` is RAM (tmpfs) — build elsewhere.\n- First build of each stack is slow: Gradle/AGP auto-install the SDK/NDK/build-tools a project pins\n  (licenses are accepted). \"SDK XML version 4 encountered\" in Gradle output is harmless.\n\n## Android (SDK `~/Android/Sdk` = `$ANDROID_HOME`, JDK 21 = `$JAVA_HOME`)\n\n- Installed: platforms android-36 + android-37.0, build-tools 37, NDK 30, cmake 4.1, emulator,\n  platform-tools, image `android-37.0;google_apis;x86_64`, AVD **`pixel8_api37`** (Android 17).\n- **Emulator (KVM, headless):**\n  `emulator -avd pixel8_api37 -no-window -no-audio -no-boot-anim -gpu swiftshader_indirect -no-snapshot &`\n  then poll `adb shell getprop sys.boot_completed` until `1` (~30 s). Stop: `adb emu kill`.\n  Needs the `kvm` group (new shells have it; an old shell: `/usr/bin/sg kvm -c '…'` — plain `sg` is\n  ast-grep here). Check: `emulator -accel-check`.\n- **`android` CLI (official, agent-first; read the `android-cli` skill).** `~/.androidrc` pins `--sdk`\n  and `--no-metrics`. `sdkmanager` still works but is deprecated. Verified:\n  - `android create empty-activity --name=\"My App\" --application-id=com.example.myapp --output=./my-app`\n  - `android run` does **not** build: `./gradlew assembleDebug --max-workers=8` first, then\n    `android run --device=emulator-5554 --apks=app/build/outputs/apk/debug/app-debug.apk`\n    (`android describe --project_dir=.` prints artifact paths).\n  - `android screen capture --device=emulator-5554 -o shot.png` · `android layout --device=emulator-5554 --pretty`\n    (UI tree JSON with bounds/centers) · `android docs search \"<query>\"` (first run indexes ~5k docs).\n- Without the CLI: `adb exec-out screencap -p > shot.png`, `adb shell monkey -p <pkg> -c\n  android.intent.category.LAUNCHER 1`, `adb logcat -d | tail`.\n- **Physical phone:** WSL has no USB. Wireless debugging (Android 11+): phone → Developer options →\n  Wireless debugging → `adb pair <ip>:<pair-port>` then `adb connect <ip>:<port>`. (USB passthrough\n  would need `usbipd-win` on Windows — not installed.)\n- **UI tests:** `maestro test flow.yaml` (Maestro 2.10, `~/.maestro`), or Android CLI journeys.\n\n## Flutter 3.47 / Dart 3.13 (`~/.local/opt/flutter`, analytics off)\n\n- `flutter doctor` green for Android, Linux desktop, web (Chrome). Verified:\n  `flutter create --platforms=android,linux,web app` · `flutter build apk --debug` (~2 min first\n  time) · `adb install -r build/app/outputs/flutter-apk/app-debug.apk` · `flutter build linux --release`\n  · `flutter build web` · `flutter run -d emulator-5554`.\n- Windows/macOS/iOS targets need those OSes — build them in CI. Upgrade: `flutter upgrade`.\n\n## React Native / Expo (Expo SDK 57, RN 0.86)\n\n- `eas-cli` global; Expo/RN CLIs per project via `npx`. Verified:\n  `npx --yes create-expo-app@latest app --template blank-typescript --yes` →\n  `CI=1 npx expo prebuild -p android --no-install` →\n  `cd android && ./gradlew assembleDebug --max-workers=8 -PreactNativeArchitectures=x86_64`\n  (x86_64 only = emulator; skips 3 of 4 ABIs).\n- **A debug APK has no bundled JS:** start Metro (`CI=1 npx expo start --port 8081 &`) and\n  `adb reverse tcp:8081 tcp:8081` before launching, else red \"Unable to load script\". Or `npx expo run:android`.\n- iOS: `eas build -p ios` (cloud; needs the user's Expo + Apple accounts).\n\n## Native Kotlin / Compose / KMP\n\n- `gradle` 9.7, `kotlin` 2.4 (mise), JDK 21. Prefer the project's `./gradlew`. `android create`\n  default template is AGP 9 / Compose. Compose Multiplatform desktop (JVM) builds here; iOS needs macOS CI.\n\n## Desktop: Tauri v2 (Rust + WebView)\n\n- Verified: `npm create tauri-app@latest app -- --template vanilla-ts --manager npm --identifier dev.example.app --yes`\n  → `npm install` → `CARGO_BUILD_JOBS=8 npx tauri build --bundles deb` (~2 min) → run the binary under\n  `xvfb-run -a`.\n- **Windows installer from WSL:** `npx tauri build --runner cargo-xwin --target x86_64-pc-windows-msvc`\n  → `.exe` + NSIS `*-setup.exe` (unsigned; MSI not possible on Linux). First run caches the MSVC CRT +\n  Windows SDK in `~/.cache/cargo-xwin` (1.1 GB). `llvm-rc`/`lld-link`/`makensis` already resolve.\n- Tauri mobile: rust android targets + NDK installed (`npx tauri android init` / `build`).\n\n## Desktop: Electron (Electron 44 via Forge)\n\n- **npm 12 refuses git-URL deps; Forge needs one** (`@electron/node-gyp`) → `EALLOWGIT`. Scaffold with\n  `npm_config_allow_git=all npx --yes create-electron-app@latest app --template=vite-typescript`, then put\n  `allow-git=all` in the project `.npmrc` (needed for every later `npm install`/`npm ci`).\n- Package: `npx electron-forge make --targets @electron-forge/maker-deb,@electron-forge/maker-zip` —\n  the **RPM maker is broken with rpm 6** (`cp: cannot stat 'usr/*'`), so plain `npm run make` fails.\n- Electron has no install script (binary downloads on first run). Check: `xvfb-run -a timeout 10 npx electron . --no-sandbox`.\n\n## .NET 10 (`~/.dotnet`)\n\n- Verified: `dotnet new avalonia.app -o App` → `dotnet build` → `dotnet publish -c Release -r win-x64\n  --self-contained` (Windows `.exe` from Linux). `dotnet run` killed by `timeout` prints a harmless\n  `ObjectDisposedException`; run the built app directly instead. LSP: `csharp-ls`.\n- MAUI on Linux = Android target only (`dotnet workload install maui-android`, not installed).\n\n## Windows binaries from WSL (build here, run console ones straight from WSL)\n\n- Rust: `cargo build --release --target x86_64-pc-windows-gnu` (mingw-w64) or `cargo xwin build\n  --target x86_64-pc-windows-msvc`. Go: `GOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go build -o app.exe .`.\n  .NET: `-r win-x64`. Tauri: see above. Flutter/WinUI/MAUI-Windows need the Windows side.\n- Windows-native toolchains (VS 2026 + Build Tools 2022, Windows dotnet/node/python) are reachable via\n  `cmd.exe /c \"…\"` / `powershell.exe -NoProfile -Command \"…\"` — run from a `/mnt/c/...` directory,\n  not a `\\\\wsl$` UNC path.\n\n## npm 12 (blocks install scripts AND git deps by default)\n\n```bash\nnpm approve-scripts --allow-scripts-pending   # which deps had install scripts skipped (read-only)\nnpm approve-scripts <pkg>                     # writes \"allowScripts\" in package.json (version-pinned)\nnpm rebuild <pkg>                             # run the skipped script now\necho 'allow-git=all' >> .npmrc                # allow git-URL dependencies (EALLOWGIT)\n```\n\nOnce a project declares `allowScripts`, npm ignores the user-level `allow-scripts` for it (it warns).\nGlobal/one-off: `npm i -g <pkg> --allow-scripts=<pkg>`. pnpm (`onlyBuiltDependencies`) and bun\n(`trustedDependencies`) use the same opt-in model.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
