{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_150","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat > PROGRESS.md <<'EOF'\n# B-17 Cross Channel Chaos — complete\n\nThe requested game and handoff are complete. No required implementation work remains.\n\n## Deliverables\n\n- \"'`B-17-Cross-Channel-Chaos-B-17-Bomber-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261106-043517.html` — finished self-contained game in this folder’s root; 4,904,338 bytes. Opens with file://, no adjacent assets, install, CDN or server required for standalone play.\n- `exit-interview.json` — exact TASK schema, parsed and matched to the game’s embedded `content/credits.json`.\n- `B-17-Cross-Channel-Chaos-B-17-Bomber-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261106-043517/` — complete source, pinned native engine/dependencies, content, build/test tools, research, handbook and evidence. See its README.md.\n- Artifact SHA256: `fdb98be625f8249b3667ba6ceaad98f1a4bda925a54aa0ddc9647cbbf55f3728`.\n\n## What shipped\n\nOperation Copper Tide is a complete roughly 10–12-minute sortie: route/payload/skill briefing, controllable assisted takeoff, shared heavy-aircraft flight/resources, four directional guns, fighters, evasive flak, finite ballistic bombing, target components/optional objectives, consequential subsystems/emergencies, return, physical assisted landing, debrief and fresh-seed replay. Includes keyboard/mouse, standard pad support, touch assistance, two local native views, AI Hub shared-world 1–2 crew/playerless host, three practice scenarios, pause/settings/handbook, dev tools/live tuning/mission JSON authoring, procedural audio and isolated ten-second attract demonstration.\n\nThe title was chosen with Node crypto.randomInt from 50 silently authored riffs (draw 42). Research follows the original instructions/developer history and directly inspected source screenshots; no original pixels or recordings are included in handoff assets.\n\n## Final verification\n\nFrom the dev folder: `npm run verify`. It rebuilds quietly and runs all suites, printing only failures and one count line.\n\nFinal exact-artifact result: **579 checks, 0 failures**, exit 0; all 14 aggregate suite entries passed. Evidence is in `docs/verify/last-run.json` and `docs/verification-summary.json`.\n\n- Simulation: 141 checks, all nine payload/difficulty whole sorties and physical runway endpoints.\n- Audio: 37 OfflineAudioContext checks.\n- Browser/UI: 223 assertions across nine scenarios, including Chrome/WebKit native WebGL, pad/mixed co-op, glyphs, pause/menu ownership, dev/editor, replay/navigation, touch holds/multitouch, attract exits, copied-file offline startup and Firefox support screen.\n- Focused native split rendering: 102 checks in Chrome and WebKit, repeated frames, pause, handover and solo recovery after departure.\n- Public native keyboard/mouse whole sortie: 8 checks; mission complete at 619 active seconds, 32% fuel, 86% hull, four fighters, two bombs, sixteen actual keyboard evasion moves, no cheats.\n- Hub: all five scenarios, 66 admission checks, zero warnings; normal, 150 ms latency/50 ms jitter/5% loss, guest leave, host leave, playerless server. All nine terminal copies remained stopped, muted, silent and send-free after actual Escape input; hosts report once.\n- Native engine source has no edits. Independent native camera-space overlays and per-viewport passes keep both first-person views stable. Standard missing GPU constants allow native WebGL capability checks before unsupported-browser messaging.\n- 40 implementation issues were corrected, four critical. Closed ledger: `docs/issues.md`; test-fixture mistakes excluded.\n\n## Confirmed limits and cleanup\n\nNative WebGL2 is the default. Software WebGPU lost its device in this environment; the cause is unknown. Installed Firefox has no usable WebGL2 and shows a useful support screen. No physical controller, real phone/tablet/TV, second human or listening validation is claimed; prescribed stand-ins were used.\n\nAll owned browser processes and ephemeral loopback test servers were closed. No neighboring runs were changed, no fixed ports were used, and no external publishing or messaging was performed. Usage is `added on import`.\nEOF\npython3 - <<'\"'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('B-17-Cross-Channel-Chaos-B-17-Bomber-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261106-043517/README.md');s=p.read_text().replace('\"'`verify` is the quiet entry point:'\"', '\"'`verify` rebuilds first and is the quiet entry point:'\"');p.write_text(s)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_150","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat > PROGRESS.md <<'EOF'\n# B-17 Cross Channel Chaos — complete\n\nThe requested game and handoff are complete. No required implementation work remains.\n\n## Deliverables\n\n- \"'`B-17-Cross-Channel-Chaos-B-17-Bomber-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261106-043517.html` — finished self-contained game in this folder’s root; 4,904,338 bytes. Opens with file://, no adjacent assets, install, CDN or server required for standalone play.\n- `exit-interview.json` — exact TASK schema, parsed and matched to the game’s embedded `content/credits.json`.\n- `B-17-Cross-Channel-Chaos-B-17-Bomber-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261106-043517/` — complete source, pinned native engine/dependencies, content, build/test tools, research, handbook and evidence. See its README.md.\n- Artifact SHA256: `fdb98be625f8249b3667ba6ceaad98f1a4bda925a54aa0ddc9647cbbf55f3728`.\n\n## What shipped\n\nOperation Copper Tide is a complete roughly 10–12-minute sortie: route/payload/skill briefing, controllable assisted takeoff, shared heavy-aircraft flight/resources, four directional guns, fighters, evasive flak, finite ballistic bombing, target components/optional objectives, consequential subsystems/emergencies, return, physical assisted landing, debrief and fresh-seed replay. Includes keyboard/mouse, standard pad support, touch assistance, two local native views, AI Hub shared-world 1–2 crew/playerless host, three practice scenarios, pause/settings/handbook, dev tools/live tuning/mission JSON authoring, procedural audio and isolated ten-second attract demonstration.\n\nThe title was chosen with Node crypto.randomInt from 50 silently authored riffs (draw 42). Research follows the original instructions/developer history and directly inspected source screenshots; no original pixels or recordings are included in handoff assets.\n\n## Final verification\n\nFrom the dev folder: `npm run verify`. It rebuilds quietly and runs all suites, printing only failures and one count line.\n\nFinal exact-artifact result: **579 checks, 0 failures**, exit 0; all 14 aggregate suite entries passed. Evidence is in `docs/verify/last-run.json` and `docs/verification-summary.json`.\n\n- Simulation: 141 checks, all nine payload/difficulty whole sorties and physical runway endpoints.\n- Audio: 37 OfflineAudioContext checks.\n- Browser/UI: 223 assertions across nine scenarios, including Chrome/WebKit native WebGL, pad/mixed co-op, glyphs, pause/menu ownership, dev/editor, replay/navigation, touch holds/multitouch, attract exits, copied-file offline startup and Firefox support screen.\n- Focused native split rendering: 102 checks in Chrome and WebKit, repeated frames, pause, handover and solo recovery after departure.\n- Public native keyboard/mouse whole sortie: 8 checks; mission complete at 619 active seconds, 32% fuel, 86% hull, four fighters, two bombs, sixteen actual keyboard evasion moves, no cheats.\n- Hub: all five scenarios, 66 admission checks, zero warnings; normal, 150 ms latency/50 ms jitter/5% loss, guest leave, host leave, playerless server. All nine terminal copies remained stopped, muted, silent and send-free after actual Escape input; hosts report once.\n- Native engine source has no edits. Independent native camera-space overlays and per-viewport passes keep both first-person views stable. Standard missing GPU constants allow native WebGL capability checks before unsupported-browser messaging.\n- 40 implementation issues were corrected, four critical. Closed ledger: `docs/issues.md`; test-fixture mistakes excluded.\n\n## Confirmed limits and cleanup\n\nNative WebGL2 is the default. Software WebGPU lost its device in this environment; the cause is unknown. Installed Firefox has no usable WebGL2 and shows a useful support screen. No physical controller, real phone/tablet/TV, second human or listening validation is claimed; prescribed stand-ins were used.\n\nAll owned browser processes and ephemeral loopback test servers were closed. No neighboring runs were changed, no fixed ports were used, and no external publishing or messaging was performed. Usage is `added on import`.\nEOF\npython3 - <<'\"'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np=Path('B-17-Cross-Channel-Chaos-B-17-Bomber-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261106-043517/README.md');s=p.read_text().replace('\"'`verify` is the quiet entry point:'\"', '\"'`verify` rebuilds first and is the quiet entry point:'\"');p.write_text(s)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_151","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"tail -18 docs/testing.md && tail -12 docs/acceptance.md && python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport hashlib,json\np=Path('../B-17-Cross-Channel-Chaos-B-17-Bomber-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261106-043517.html')\nassert hashlib.sha256(p.read_bytes()).hexdigest()=='fdb98be625f8249b3667ba6ceaad98f1a4bda925a54aa0ddc9647cbbf55f3728'\nassert json.loads(Path('../exit-interview.json').read_text())==json.loads(Path('content/credits.json').read_text())\nprint('Final artifact and metadata unchanged; JSON valid.')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_151","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"tail -18 docs/testing.md && tail -12 docs/acceptance.md && python3 - <<'PY'\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport hashlib,json\np=Path('../B-17-Cross-Channel-Chaos-B-17-Bomber-gpt-6.1-sol-ultra-20261106-043517.html')\nassert hashlib.sha256(p.read_bytes()).hexdigest()=='fdb98be625f8249b3667ba6ceaad98f1a4bda925a54aa0ddc9647cbbf55f3728'\nassert json.loads(Path('../exit-interview.json').read_text())==json.loads(Path('content/credits.json').read_text())\nprint('Final artifact and metadata unchanged; JSON valid.')\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"\nThe first native-input browser bot destroyed the primary and four fighters but did not evade return flak or use its extinguisher, so it lost the aircraft on return. The integrating builder extended that input script with the same defensive decisions as the successful simulation bot. The corrected run passed **8/8 browser-playthrough assertions** at **619 active seconds**, with **32% fuel**, **86% hull**, **four fighters**, **two bombs**, and **sixteen actual keyboard evasion moves**, without cheats. The failed initial script was an incomplete fixture and is excluded from game issue totals. The resulting recovery review discovered that debrief could precede physical runway touchdown; authored recovery interpolation now corrects this, and the subsequent nine-sortie physical aircraft-state regressions all pass.\n\nMost helpful simulation test: the complete nine-combination operation, because it ties flight, ownership, aiming, damage, finite ordnance, target consequences and return reserves together. Isolated formula checks are useful for exact impact math but cannot establish playability by themselves.\n\n## Integration audit follow-up\n\nThe read-only requirement audit found pilot-look plumbing, fine/coarse sight presentation, persistent independent voice volume, free-camera input wiring, missing touch firing/jettison paths, local Player2 map command identity, indirect pilot command transfer during seat exchange, repeated station-request radio flooding, and the dogleg's initially cosmetic risk description. These were corrected and covered by the simulation, audio and final browser suites. Emergency jettison now captures/cancels the held pointer correctly: releasing outside the original button cancels the irreversible hold. The browser suite verifies both cancellation and deliberate completion.\n\nGuest gunfire sound was an additional confirmed integration issue: mount shots rendered and consumed ammo, but the transport's intentionally trimmed event stream omitted their audio. The monotonic-counter presentation fallback and regression checks described above correct that without changing authority or packet rate.\n\nDeveloper visualization now draws actual target/fighter collision volumes, swept shot segments, attack paths and authored encounter-trigger planes through the native pen. Mission authoring, deterministic numeric seed input, state/definition export and live parameters are complete. The canonical count/severity record is `issues.md`; this narrative does not introduce a second issue total.\n\nIssue 38 is closed by the native camera-space overlay proof above. Issue 39 retains the standalone title/results screen when native Back/Options would otherwise hide its sole menu; Hub, attract and pending scene transitions are excluded. Issue 40 carries the Review briefing intent through the queued native title-scene entry, preventing that briefing from being replaced by the title menu. Both final navigation corrections pass the dedicated 32-assertion menu regression. The engine files remain unchanged.\n\n## Confirmed limits\n\nThe installed software WebGPU route was attempted and produced device-loss/buffer errors. It is not a passing renderer claim; the release uses the tested native WebGL 2 backend by default. Hardware WebGPU behavior is unknown. No physical controller, touch device, second human, real iPhone/iPad, television or listener was available. Device profiles, pad stubs, multiple browser clients and Web Audio measurements are the prescribed stand-ins. Exact original audio timbres were not audited; the shipped score, effects and invented voices are original. Standalone remote room hosting is not implemented or advertised: remote cooperation uses AI Hub, while standalone supports solo and local two-person play.\n9. Same-station requests initially reset aim and repeatedly emitted radio messages; now harmless no-ops, with a regression covering repeated requests.\n10. Trigger/path/hit-volume visualization initially exceeded what `debugVolumes` drew; native pen geometry now includes real target/fighter hit volumes, swept shot segments, attack paths and authored encounter-trigger planes, with final debug screenshots.\n11. High-rate gun events were deliberately absent from compact Hub snapshots, silencing guests' gun sound. The audio presentation now detects authoritative mount-shot increments when no matching new gun event was heard. Regression tests confirm audible guest fire, no host doubling and no terminal sound leak.\n12. Assisted recovery initially triggered debrief before physical runway touchdown. Authored twelve-second safe-gate interpolation now reaches the runway, deploys gear and stops the aircraft; all nine full-sortie final aircraft-state assertions pass.\n\nFindings 1–12 above are closed construction findings. Browser tests verify their input consequences; authoritative simulation and audio regressions cover shared state rules. Issue 38 is closed by independent native camera-space overlays and repeated Chromium/WebKit retention proof. Issue 39 retains the sole standalone title/results menu outside Hub/attract/transitions. Issue 40 carries Review briefing intent through queued native scene entry. Both final navigation changes pass the dedicated 32-assertion menu regression. All 40 implementation issues in the canonical ledger are corrected; four were critical.\n\n## Confirmed limits and final hardening\n\nNative WebGL 2 works in the installed Chromium and WebKit browsers. Software WebGPU was attempted and lost its device/failed buffers; it is not claimed as passing, and hardware WebGPU behavior is unknown. The installed Firefox has no usable WebGL 2 and displays a tested explanatory screen. No physical pad, touch device, second person, real iPhone/iPad, TV or listener was available; prescribed stubs, profiles, multi-client pages and measured audio were used. Original source audio recordings were not audited. Standalone internet hosting is not advertised; remote co-op is provided by AI Hub.\n\nThe final strengthened five-scenario Hub rerun passed all 66 admission checks with zero warnings. It additionally presses native Escape after terminal cleanup: all nine copies keep the native loop stopped, audio muted, no sound sources and no subsequent sends; authoritative hosts finish once. `hub-final-summary.json` records the tested artifact hash and links the full `hub-results.json`. Native split-foreground and final navigation proofs are separately complete. The complete final `npm run verify` is currently running against the exact artifact; its final result will replace this in-progress verification note.\nFinal artifact and metadata unchanged; JSON valid.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
